# ==============================================================================================
# Custodexa environment template (shared by the dev and production compose files)
# ==============================================================================================
# This English file is the only template actually used: `cp .env.example .env` copies this one.
# Translations exist so you can read the explanations in another language, but the file you copy
# and edit is always this one, never a translation:
#   Traditional Chinese: docs/zh-TW/env-example.md
#   Japanese:            docs/ja-JP/env-example.md
#
# Setup, same for operators and developers:
#   1. cp .env.example .env
#   2. Edit: change every secret, then set DB_SSLMODE / LDAP / DATA_PATH as needed
#   3. docker compose up -d
# Or in one command: `bash scripts/quickstart.sh --up` creates .env, generates the secrets still
# left at their template values, and starts the stack; values you already filled in are untouched.
#
# The default docker-compose.yml is the production stack: nginx serves the compiled frontend, the
# backend is a slim binary, no test targets. Deploying Custodexa for your own use? Done here.
#
# Developers: uncomment COMPOSE_FILE below and every `docker compose` command (including
# `exec ... go test`) targets the development stack without `-f` -- hot reload for frontend and
# backend, the full Go toolchain, six test targets (ssh, rdp, vnc, ldap, mysql, k3s). An explicit
# `-f docker-compose.yml` still overrides it, so production stays verifiable from a dev machine.
# Set KEK_PROVIDER=env with an ENCRYPTION_KEY too: hot reload restarts the process on every
# rebuild, and `ui` would make you unseal after every save.
#
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.dev.yml
#
# The line above must stay commented out. Copying this template is what gives a deployer the
# production stack; a live value here would silently hand them the development stack instead
# (Vite dev server, different published ports). COMPOSE_FILE is consumed by the docker compose
# CLI rather than by the backend, so it stays outside the env drift guard's key set.
#
# Both compose files read this file through `env_file`, and compose uses ${DATA_PATH} to place
# persistent data. Careful: `env_file` does not strip an inline comment from an EMPTY value (the
# line `SOME_KEY=  # note` yields the literal value "# note"), and most knobs here default to
# empty -- so every explanation sits on its own line and no value line carries a trailing comment.
# Topology and mode constants (DB_HOST=postgres, GUACD_HOST=guacd, DB_DRIVER, GIN_MODE, container
# paths) come from the compose `environment:` block, which outranks `env_file`; setting them here
# does nothing. DB_DRIVER has no factory default and the backend refuses to start without it: both
# compose files supply it, but a bare-binary or self-built deployment (k8s manifest, systemd unit)
# must supply it there. PostgreSQL is the only supported target -- the sqlite branch serves unit
# tests, and the versioned migrations are PostgreSQL SQL.
# This is the only environment template, and backend/config/env_drift_test.go fails the build if a
# variable the backend consumes is neither documented here nor supplied by compose.
#
# Markers: [secret] change before production; [PCI x.y] the PCI DSS requirement behind the key --
# when the marker reads "recommends N", N is the value that requirement names and the shipped
# default is a different one you may want to raise or lower; every other [PCI] key is a requirement
# the system enforces on its own, and the explanation on that key says what it refuses to do while
# the requirement is unmet; [seed] written once at first startup and managed from the policy page
# afterwards.
# ==============================================================================================

# ---- Data location ----
# Root folder for application data (audit logs, recordings, database), bind-mounted into the
# containers. Defaults to ./data inside the project, which is easy to inspect during development;
# in production point it at a dedicated folder or disk, e.g. /opt/custodexa/data.
DATA_PATH=./data

# ---- Database (names and credentials of the compose postgres service, dev and prod alike) ----
DB_NAME=custodexa
DB_USER=postgres
# [secret] Dev default; always replace it with a strong password in production.
DB_PASSWORD=postgres
# The bundled postgres is reached over an isolated same-host bridge without TLS, hence disable.
# An external database reached across a network needs require or verify-full (PCI Req 4).
DB_SSLMODE=disable

# ---- Secrets ----
# [secret][PCI 2.2.2] JWT_SECRET is the trust root of HS256 authentication and must be at least
# 32 bytes (shorter refuses startup). The value below is the shipped default: a release (prod)
# build refuses to start while it is unchanged (fail-close, PCI 2.2.2), a debug build accepts it.
# Generate a production value with a CSPRNG, e.g. openssl rand -base64 32.
JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production-dev-secret
#
# ---- KEK: the key-encryption key of the envelope encryption ----
# The KEK protects every encrypted column in the database: asset credentials, SFTP passwords, TOTP
# seeds, notification secrets. The shipped default is `ui`. Key material has no factory default in
# any mode, so every deployment needs one explicit action -- run the quickstart script, or generate
# and declare the material yourself. Know what each mode costs you to operate:
#
#   ui (shipped default): the material exists ONLY in memory -- generated locally in the browser on
#       the first-run initialization page, re-entered by an administrator on the unseal page after
#       that, never written to disk. The cost: EVERY process restart (a crash, a host reboot, a
#       container recreate) comes back sealed and stays out of service until a human types the key
#       in again. Unattended deployments should use env or kms.
#
#   env: set KEK_PROVIDER=env, then uncomment ENCRYPTION_KEY below and fill in a generated value
#       (`bash scripts/quickstart.sh` generates it once env is declared). Restarts need no human,
#       which is also what makes hot-reload development bearable. The cost: the material sits in
#       cleartext on the server's disk, so whoever can read this file -- through a backup, a
#       snapshot, a mistaken permission bit -- holds your root key.
#
#   kms: delegate to a cloud key service; see the settings below.
#
#   hsm: the hardware module interface is not implemented. Do not select this value.
#
# KEK_PROVIDER is case sensitive, and any value outside the whitelist (including ENV) refuses
# startup: no guessing, no fallback. Leaving it EMPTY means unset, not ui -- an empty value falls
# back to env mode when ENCRYPTION_KEY is present, and refuses startup when it is not. `ui` is this
# template's explicit shipped value, not a runtime default.
KEK_PROVIDER=ui
#
# [secret][PCI 2.2.2] KEK material: one 32-BYTE key. "32 bytes" is the key length, not the length
# of what you type, so all three of these are accepted:
#   1. exactly 32 characters (A-Z a-z 0-9)
#   2. 64 hexadecimal characters
#   3. base64 (standard or URL-safe, padded or not) that decodes to exactly 32 bytes
# Anything that does not decode to 32 bytes is rejected; short material is never padded out.
#
# The key below is deliberately left commented out. Only KEK_PROVIDER=env needs it -- under ui (the
# default) and kms it MUST stay commented, and a value there is a contradictory configuration that
# refuses startup. Generate the material with a CSPRNG instead of inventing a string: a low-entropy
# value passes the length check and is still weak, and a value copied from public documentation
# would give every deployment the same KEK. Any of these works:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   LC_ALL=C tr -dc 'A-Za-z0-9' < /dev/urandom | head -c 32
# (One command per accepted form; a guard test runs each one and checks that its output always
# passes validation.) There is exactly one KEK material key -- no alias, no fallback key -- and an
# empty string does not "disable" it: empty means no material, and env mode then refuses to start.
# Under ui, the placeholder below staying commented out is the correct state.
# To use env mode, uncomment the next line and paste your generated value:
# ENCRYPTION_KEY=
#
# Delegated mode settings (KEK_PROVIDER=kms): the three KEK_KMS_* keys are checked for completeness
# at startup and missing ones are listed individually, and every local KEK material key must stay
# unset or the configuration is contradictory and startup is refused.
#
# KEK_KMS_PROVIDER currently supports aws only. KEK_KMS_KEY_ID takes an alias, a bare key id or a
# full ARN; startup normalizes it to a key ARN through DescribeKey before storing it, so the three
# spellings are equivalent. Credentials come from the AWS SDK default chain (IRSA, instance profile,
# AWS_* variables, SSO); Custodexa neither holds cloud credentials nor offers an endpoint override.
# Endpoint overrides are refused outright on the production path: AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_KMS and
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL are resolved by the SDK itself and would send kms:Encrypt requests -- which carry
# the plaintext DEK -- to that address, possibly over plain HTTP. Either one being set is
# fail-close, and so is endpoint_url in ~/.aws/config.
# KEK_KMS_KEY_ID also declares which KMS account this deployment trusts: a rewrap target handed to
# the key-change wizard (key_ref in the request body) is rejected unless it lives in the same
# partition and the same AWS account as this key. A matching region is not enough.
# IAM actions needed: kms:DescribeKey, kms:Encrypt, kms:Decrypt; changing from one KMS key to
# another also needs kms:ReEncryptFrom and kms:ReEncryptTo (two actions, not a single
# kms:ReEncrypt). Besides DescribeKey, startup runs one throwaway Encrypt/Decrypt round trip, so
# missing permissions or an unreachable external key store (XKS) surface at boot rather than at
# first use.
# A multi-region key (MRK) works as a KEK, but this version treats primary and replica as two
# different keys (the stored kek_id includes the region), so switching to the replica is a key
# change and needs a rewrap through the wizard first.
# Migrating from a local mode (env or ui) to kms, in order: fill in the three keys below while
# still running locally, rewrap to the target key_ref in the key-change wizard, then remove the
# local KEK material key, set KEK_PROVIDER=kms and restart.
# KEK_KMS_PROVIDER=
# KEK_KMS_KEY_ID=
# KEK_KMS_REGION=
# Unused, listed for completeness (hsm mode):
# KEK_HSM_MODULE=
# KEK_HSM_TOKEN_LABEL=
# KEK_HSM_KEY_LABEL=
# KEK_HSM_PIN=
# KEK_HSM_PIN_FILE=
# [secret][PCI 8.3.6/2.2.2] ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD is the password of the initial admin account
# created on a brand-new (empty database) deployment. There is no public default any more, so every
# deployment that seeds -- a fresh dev database included -- has to set its own value. The
# placeholder below is on a denylist: an empty-database startup in any mode is refused while the
# value is that placeholder, shorter than 12 characters, or contains whitespace or a newline.
# Use a high-entropy value (openssl rand -base64 24, trailing newline stripped). It retires at the
# forced password change on first login, so remove or rotate it here once you are in; a startup
# against a non-empty database warns if a usable value is still present. An existing non-empty
# database does not need this value at all.
ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD=change-me-admin-initial-password-in-env

# ---- Server ----
# Note: GIN_MODE is fixed by compose per environment (dev=debug, prod=release). It is not a
# deployment knob, so it is not listed here.
# [PCI 7.3] Comma-separated allowlist of the origins allowed to call the API from a browser. Under
# release the list is enforced as written and a request from any other origin is refused; leaving
# it empty narrows that to the serving origin alone.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Bearer token for the operational metrics endpoint /metrics. Empty = no
# authentication, and empty is the safe default: /metrics does not live under /api, and the
# production edge only proxies /api and /ws, so nothing outside can reach it. If you open a route
# to /metrics on your reverse proxy in order to scrape it from another host, you MUST set this
# value -- otherwise you are publishing the endpoint inventory, per-feature usage and error rates.
METRICS_TOKEN=

# ---- Browser sessions ----
# [seed] Whether the session-refresh cookie handed to browsers carries the Secure attribute, which
# makes the browser keep and send it over HTTPS only. Accepted values are true and false. The value
# here seeds the security policy of the same name at first startup; from then on the switch lives
# in System Settings -> Security Policy ("Keep sign-in only on https connections"), where saving
# applies to the next cookie the system hands out and needs no restart. Editing this file once the
# policy holds a value changes nothing.
# Leave it empty to take the seed from PUBLIC_BASE_URL: an https address seeds true, an http
# address seeds false, and with neither of them set the policy keeps its shipped default of true.
# Set it to true when TLS is terminated in front of this system and PUBLIC_BASE_URL cannot carry
# the public https address; set it to false before the first startup when you serve this system
# over plain http.
# A plain-http deployment that leaves the policy on stays in service, at a price: the browser
# discards the cookie, so every user signs in again every 15 minutes -- the lifetime of an access
# token. The sign-in page says so when it happens, the security policy page carries the same note
# for administrators, and turning the policy off there ends it right away.
# Local development over http://localhost keeps working on Chromium and Firefox, which accept
# Secure cookies from that origin. WebKit-based browsers (Safari) discard them there, so on those
# you are signed out every 15 minutes until the policy is off.
# The startup log states the value in effect and where it came from.
AUTH_REFRESH_COOKIE_SECURE=

# ---- Feature flags ----
# Note: permission checks have no flag at all; they apply unconditionally in every mode.
# FEATURE_AUDIT_LOG_ENABLED is the one release safety floor here: under GIN_MODE=release, setting
# it to false is ignored and auditing is forced back on, with the forced key name listed in the
# startup log (auditing every operation is a red line and cannot be disabled by the deployment
# layer). The other four flags below are ordinary switches -- whatever you set is what you get, in
# release mode too.
FEATURE_AUDIT_LOG_ENABLED=true
FEATURE_ASYNC_AUDIT_ENABLED=true
FEATURE_AUDIT_FALLBACK_TO_FILE=true
FEATURE_ANOMALY_DETECTION_ENABLED=false
FEATURE_ALERTING_ENABLED=false

# ---- OIDC identity provider integration ----
# The providers themselves (issuer, client id, secret, admission rules) are configured in the admin
# UI and stored in the database; only the three settings the deployment layer must decide are here.
# Two operational preconditions before you enable SSO (full text in docs/QUICKSTART.md):
#
# 1. Keep at least one local admin account. Unsealing (KEK_PROVIDER=ui) and initial-admin
#    authorization accept local credentials only: they happen before the system is fully up, so no
#    external IdP can complete them. If every admin is externalized, a sealed system has nobody
#    left who can unseal it. Give that account a strong password and enable MFA. (An invariant
#    already blocks dropping the last local admin.)
#
# 2. Disabling a user at the IdP does not cut live protocol sessions. There is no back-channel
#    logout in this version, and an established SSH/RDP/VNC session no longer touches the
#    credential, so it survives until the idle timeout or the maximum session length; an issued
#    access token stays valid until it expires (15 minutes). An IdP-side suspension only denies the
#    NEXT login. To cut access immediately, disable the account or the whole provider in this
#    system's admin UI: both revoke credentials, terminate sessions and drop monitoring feeds.

# Public base URL, used to build the redirect_uri handed to the IdP and the callback return
# address. It is NOT derived from the request Host: derivation is bound to be wrong behind layered
# reverse proxies, and the wrong value ends up in redirect_uri and sends users to the wrong host.
# While it is unset, enabled providers are marked incomplete and hidden from the login page
# (fail-close). Production must be https, e.g. https://bastion.example.com.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Dedicated issuer declarations (comma separated). An unknown issuer is fail-closed as a SHARED
# identity domain, which forces its admission rules to carry an organization condition (tenant id
# or hosted domain); Okta, self-hosted IdPs and the like do not issue such a claim, so without this
# declaration their automatic provisioning cannot be configured. Declaring an issuer here means
# "this issuer serves only our organization" -- a judgement only the deployer can make, which is
# why it is not exposed to admins in the UI. The built-in shared list (Google, Microsoft
# multi-tenant endpoints and so on) wins and cannot be overridden from here. A change takes effect
# only after a rolling restart of every replica.
# Example: OIDC_DEDICATED_ISSUERS=https://acme.okta.com,https://idp.internal.example
OIDC_DEDICATED_ISSUERS=

# Internal hostnames allowed as outbound destinations (comma separated). Outbound calls to an IdP
# refuse by default to resolve to loopback, link-local (cloud metadata addresses included) or
# private ranges, so an internal IdP has to be listed here explicitly; there is no boolean that
# turns the address check off. Outside release mode, hostnames listed here may also be reached over
# http, which is what the dev IdP target needs.
OIDC_ALLOWED_INTERNAL_HOSTS=

# ---- LDAP authentication (seed only, not runtime settings) ----
# The keys from LDAP_ENABLED through LDAP_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY below are written to the database once,
# and only when the first startup finds an empty table and LDAP_ENABLED=true. After that the admin
# UI page Identity Management -> LDAP Directory is the only source of truth and editing this file
# has no effect (the system records an evaluation marker, so deleting the rows does not make it
# seed again). The last key of the section, LDAP_ALLOWED_LOOPBACK_ENDPOINTS, is not part of that
# set: it is a runtime outbound policy that keeps being read from this file.
# Before downgrading to an older version, copy the current admin-UI settings back into this file,
# bind password included; see docs/QUICKSTART.md.
# Disabled by default. To test LDAP in development set LDAP_ENABLED=true -- the values below match
# the bundled ldap-test service. In production, point LDAP_URL at a real directory server and fill
# in the matching DN and password.
LDAP_ENABLED=false
# The bundled dev test service; in production use a real server (ldap:// or ldaps://).
LDAP_URL=ldap://ldap-test:1389
LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=admin,dc=example,dc=org
# [secret] Dev test value; replace it in production.
LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=adminpass
LDAP_BASE_DN=ou=users,dc=example,dc=org
LDAP_USER_FILTER=(uid=%s)
LDAP_ATTR_EMAIL=mail
LDAP_ATTR_FULLNAME=cn
# For self-signed test certificates only; always false in production.
LDAP_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY=false
# Loopback endpoints allowed as outbound destinations (comma separated, exact host:port match, no
# wildcards). LDAP dialing refuses loopback, link-local (cloud metadata addresses included),
# unspecified and multicast addresses by default, while private ranges are allowed because a
# directory normally lives on the internal network; there is no boolean that turns the address
# check off. List the endpoint here when the directory runs on the same host, e.g. 127.0.0.1:1389.
LDAP_ALLOWED_LOOPBACK_ENDPOINTS=

# ---- Session timeouts (minutes) ----
# [seed] Seeded at first startup; managed from the security policy page afterwards.
# [PCI 8.2.8 recommends 15]; 0 = disabled.
SSH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=30
# 0 = unlimited.
SSH_MAX_SESSION_MINUTES=0

# ---- Audit, recording and operational limits ----
# [PCI 10.5.1 recommends 365][seed] Empty = the policy default of 90.
RECORDING_RETENTION_DAYS=
# [seed] Checkpoint sealing interval in seconds; empty = 3600, maximum 86400 (24 hours). Zero, an
# invalid value or one out of range falls back to the default. After the first startup, System
# Settings -> Security Policy is authoritative and this is only the initial value (env no longer
# takes part once the policy page has been used).
AUDIT_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=
# [seed] Checkpoint sealing row threshold; empty, invalid or out of range = 10000, maximum 1000000.
# Whichever of the threshold and the interval is reached first triggers the checkpoint.
AUDIT_CHECKPOINT_ROW_THRESHOLD=
# [seed] Maximum re-encryptions per key-change run; empty or invalid = 100000.
KEY_ROTATION_MAX_PER_RUN=
# [seed] Maximum deletions per retention cleanup run; empty or invalid = 100000.
RETENTION_MAX_PER_RUN=
# [seed] Kubernetes list timeout in seconds; empty or invalid = 10.
K8S_LIST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# ---- Seal state machine and unseal endpoint (in effect when KEK_PROVIDER=ui) ----
# Every key here may be left empty, in which case the built-in safe defaults apply. KEK material
# reaches memory only through the unseal API, so this whole section is rate, source and listener
# knobs and carries no key material. The seal-period journal is written to the audit log directory:
# both compose files set AUDIT_LOG_PATH=/var/log/custodexa/audit in the container and bind-mount it
# from ${DATA_PATH}/audit on the host, which is where you will find the journal. There is no
# separate key for its location.
#
# The four *_SECONDS knobs below share one parsing rule: empty or non-numeric text falls back to
# the default named on the key, but a NUMBER outside 1..86400 -- zero, negative or oversized --
# refuses startup instead of being clamped, because a rate limit that silently ignored your value
# would be worse than none. Startup is likewise refused when a ceiling is lower than its base.
# Per-source backoff base in seconds; empty or non-numeric = 2.
SEAL_UNSEAL_BACKOFF_BASE_SECONDS=
# Per-source backoff ceiling in seconds; empty or non-numeric = 300. The growth of the backoff
# needs a ceiling so that "wait and you can try again" stays true at any attack rate.
SEAL_UNSEAL_BACKOFF_MAX_SECONDS=
# Consecutive unseal attempts carrying the wrong key that arm the global cooldown. The count runs
# across all sources together and the first successful unseal resets it to zero. This is a number
# of attempts, not a duration, so it does not compare with the *_SECONDS knobs above: set it well
# above the run of mistakes one administrator can plausibly make, so a mistyped key costs that one
# person a per-source wait instead of putting the endpoint into a cooldown that also holds up
# everyone else. Empty = 20. Unlike the *_SECONDS knobs above, any other value that is not an
# integer in 1..1048576 -- non-numeric text included -- refuses startup rather than falling back to
# the default.
SEAL_UNSEAL_COOLDOWN_THRESHOLD=
# Global cooldown base in seconds; empty or non-numeric = 60. A cooldown always expires on its own;
# recovering from one never requires restarting the process.
SEAL_UNSEAL_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=
# Global cooldown ceiling in seconds; empty or non-numeric = 900.
SEAL_UNSEAL_COOLDOWN_MAX_SECONDS=
# Trusted proxy list (IP or CIDR, comma separated). This one reaches past the unseal endpoint: it
# decides which client IP every request is attributed to, so it also governs the address recorded
# in the audit log and the key the login rate limit counts against. Set it whenever Custodexa runs
# behind a reverse proxy, whichever KEK_PROVIDER you chose. While it is unset, per-IP backoff
# degrades to global backoff: without an agreed proxy chain the rate-limit key can be poisoned
# through forwarding headers, and a bypassable defense is worse than a blunt one.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=
# Separate listen address for the unseal endpoint, e.g. 127.0.0.1:8081; empty = share the main
# service listener.
SEAL_UNSEAL_BIND_ADDR=
# Source ranges allowed to reach the unseal endpoint (CIDR or bare IP, comma separated); empty = no
# restriction. Pair it with your management network: on an unauthenticated endpoint, no rate-limit
# measure can guarantee that an administrator gets through.
SEAL_UNSEAL_ALLOWED_CIDRS=
